  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q37-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "When $x^4+ax^3+x^2+ax+1$ is divided by $x^2+1$, the remainder is always a fixed constant, regardless of the value of $a$. Find this constant.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$1$.",
      "worked": "Successive subtraction gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^4+ax^3+x^2+ax+1-x^2(x^2+1)&=ax^3+ax+1\\\\\nax^3+ax+1-ax(x^2+1)&=1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$x^4+ax^3+x^2+ax+1=(x^2+1)(x^2+ax)+1.$$\nThe remainder is $1$, independent of $a$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
